const body = `<h1>9.4a — Interfaces: Contracts, Not Parents</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>


<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Inheritance means IS-A. A Goblin IS-A Creature. But what about IS-ABLE-TO? A Goblin CAN-FIGHT. A Dragon CAN-FIGHT. A Player CAN-FIGHT. None of them share a parent that makes sense for "fighting" — but they all need <code>attack()</code> and <code>takeDamage()</code>. An <strong>interface</strong> is a contract: "any class that implements me MUST provide these methods." No fields, no constructors, no inheritance — just method signatures.</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment: Your First Interface</h3>
        <p>Create <code>Combatant.java</code> (an interface, NOT a class):</p>
        <pre><code>public interface Combatant {
    int attack();
    void takeDamage(int damage);
    boolean isAlive();
    String getName();
}</code></pre>
        <p>Now make Creature implement it:</p>
        <pre><code>public class Creature implements Combatant {
    // Creature already HAS these methods — no changes needed!
    // But now you can also make Player implement Combatant
}</code></pre>
        <p>Make Player implement Combatant too. Now write polymorphic code:</p>
        <pre><code>Combatant[] fighters = new Combatant[3];
fighters[0] = new Goblin();
fighters[1] = new Dragon();
fighters[2] = new Player("Hero", 100, 15);

for (Combatant c : fighters) {
    System.out.println(c.getName() + " attacks: " + c.attack());
}</code></pre>
        <p>Player and Creature share NO parent class (well, Object). But they both implement Combatant, so they're both valid here.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Interface ≠ Parent Class:</strong> An interface CANNOT have instance variables, constructors, or method bodies (before Java 8). It's pure method signatures. A class can implement MULTIPLE interfaces. Read the <a href="../docs/unit-09/object-interfaces-casting.html">Comparison Interfaces docs</a> for the full interface rules and the Comparable pattern.
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build: Comparable</h3>
        <p>Make Item implement <code>Comparable&lt;Item&gt;</code>:</p>
        <pre><code>public class Item implements Comparable&lt;Item&gt; {
    // ... existing fields and methods ...

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Item other) {
        return this.value - other.value;  // sort by value ascending
    }
}</code></pre>
        <p>Now <code>Collections.sort(inventory)</code> works. Try it with your DynamicInventory. Then change compareTo to sort by name alphabetically instead.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Could you have used an abstract class instead of Combatant? What's the difference?</li>
            <li>Why can you implement multiple interfaces but only extend one class?</li>
            <li>Read the <a href="../docs/unit-09/object-interfaces-casting.html">docs section on Comparator vs Comparable</a>. When would you use one over the other?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Define an interface with method signatures</li>
            <li>Implement an interface on multiple unrelated classes</li>
            <li>Write polymorphic code using interface references</li>
            <li>Implement Comparable for natural sort order</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/object-interfaces-casting.html">Comparison Interfaces</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/polymorphism.html">Polymorphism</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/extends-and-super.html">Inheritance Basics</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
